// DIA-48447 error solution


DIA-48447: The input path does not contain any ADR homes
 Oracle Database Tips by Donald BurlesonMay 20, 2016 


Question: What do I do to resolve the DIA-48447 error?

adrci> set homepath /opt/oracle/admin/PDS_OR3/diag/rdbms/ulfp/PDS_OR3

DIA-48447: The input path [/opt/oracle/admin/PDS_OR3/diag/rdbms/ulfp/PDS_OR3] does not contain any ADR homes

Answer:  The oerr utility shows this for the DIA-48447 error:

DIA-48447: The input path [string] does not contain any ADR homes

 Cause: The input path does not contain ADR homes

Action: Validate the path

In this case you need to issue the adrci "set base" command:

adrci> set base /u01/app/oracle 

################################################################################################################################################################
Insert Fails With ORA-14300 On Partition Table (Doc ID 1472941.1)

ORA-14300: partitioning key maps to a partition outside maximum permitted number of partitions
14300. 00000 -  "partitioning key maps to a partition outside maximum permitted number of partitions"
*Cause:    The row inserted had a partitioning key that maps to a partition number greater than 1048575
           *Action  Ensure that the partitioning key falls within 1048575 partitions or subpartitions.

https://support.oracle.com/epmos/faces/DocumentDisplay?_afrLoop=345712375313132&parent=EXTERNAL_SEARCH&sourceId=PROBLEM&id=1472941.1&_afrWindowMode=0&_adf.ctrl-state=119j4we6xm_4


In this Document
Symptoms
Changes
Cause
Solution
References

APPLIES TO:
Oracle Database - Enterprise Edition - Version 11.1.0.6 and later
Oracle Database Cloud Schema Service - Version N/A and later
Oracle Database Exadata Express Cloud Service - Version N/A and later
Oracle Database Exadata Cloud Machine - Version N/A and later
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure - Database Service - Version N/A and later
Information in this document applies to any platform.
SYMPTOMS
NOTE: In the images and/or the document content below, the user information and data used represents fictitious data from the Oracle sample schema(s) or Public Documentation delivered with an Oracle database product.  Any similarity to actual persons, living or dead, is purely coincidental and not intended in any manner. 
The error occurs on interval partitioned table.

The required partition was not added automatically due to the user gets ORA-14300: partitioning key maps to a partition outside maximum permitted number of partitions error message.

CHANGES
 

CAUSE
 

ORA-14300 will be raised when the partitioning key falls beyond 1048575 partitions or subpartitions.

As it is documented in the Database Error Messages document:
ORA-14300: partitioning key maps to a partition outside maximum permitted number of partitions
Cause: The row inserted had a partitioning key that maps to a partition number greater than 1048575
Action: Ensure that the partitioning key falls within 1048575 partitions or subpartitions.
 

The number of partitions is a computed value from the start value and the given interval.

Even if there is currently fewer physical partitions created, it fails with the ORA-14300 error.

This is because we reserve those partitions for the time when the corresponding data will be inserted in that range.

 

1) To understand this allocation check the DDL of the given table.

SPOOL TEST.txt
SET LONG 2000
SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL( 'TABLE','&TABLE_NAME','&OWNER') FROM DUAL;
SPOOL OFF
 

In the above DDL read through the section that describes the partition key and the interval.

for example

CREATE TABLE TEST
  (
REGION_REPORT_ID NUMBER NOT NULL ENABLE                                ---> column definition
   )
 PARTITION BY RANGE ("REGION_REPORT_ID") INTERVAL (1)                  ---> interval partition
(PARTITION "TEST_20061008"  VALUES LESS THAN (2006100901));

-- row for 08/10/2006
insert into TEST values(2006100801);  ---> this goes into existing range partition TEST_20061008

-- row for 09/10/2006
insert into TEST values(2006100901);  --->  this goes into new interval partition, automatically created at the time of the insert

Note between the partition TEST_20061008 and the

Table             PARTITION_NAME            HIGH_VALUE
----------------- ------------------------- ----------
TEST              TEST_20061008             2006100901
TEST              SYS_P5313                 2006100902

-- row for 10/10/2006
insert into TEST values(2006101001);  --->  this goes into new interval partition, automatically created at the time of the insert

Table             PARTITION_NAME            HIGH_VALUE
----------------- ------------------------- ----------
TEST              TEST_20061008             2006100901
TEST              SYS_P5313                 2006100902
TEST              SYS_P5314                 2006101002 --> note this partition is 100 intervals apart from the previous partition though it belongs to next day
In the above example, it shows that the partition key is a Number column, the interval increments by 1 number.

 

2) Then query the minum value that explains where the INTERVAL starts. And compute the value for the newly inserted value.

Though the inserted rows are subsequent days in the calendar, due to the implementation of the value of the partition key in the above business scenario,

the corresponding interval partitions are 100 intervals apart (2006101002 - 2006100902 = 100 intervals "reserved" between partitions SYS_P5314 and SYS_P5313)

In a year we reserve 2007100902-2006100902 = 1 000 000, which then easily leads to the error:

insert into TEST values(2008050902)
            *
ERROR at line 1:
ORA-14300: partitioning key maps to a partition outside maximum permitted number of partitions
This issue has been discussed in the following bug and closed as not a bug.

Bug 13604209 ORA-14300 TRYING TO INSERT INTO INTERVAL PARTITION TABLE
 

Note you can also get ORA-14300 when trying to insert NULL value to the partition key of an interval partition table. This scenario can be seen in the example below.
 

SH@dw23>   create table t_part (col_date date)
  2   PARTITION BY RANGE (col_date)
  3   interval(numtodsinterval(1,'day'))
  4   (PARTITION P_200105 VALUES LESS THAN (TO_DATE(' 2001-06-01 00:00:00', 'SYYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS', 'NLS_CALENDAR=GREGORIAN')));

Table created.

SH@dw23>
SH@dw23>  insert into t_part (col_date) values (null);
 insert into t_part (col_date) values (null)
             *
ERROR at line 1:
ORA-14300: partitioning key maps to a partition outside maximum permitted number of partitions

 

 

 

SOLUTION
Make sure there is no attempt to insert NULL value to the partition key of an interval partition table.

Other than that you could disable interval partitioning and create the partitions manually (or schedule a job).

ALTER TABLE "SCHEMA"."T1" SET INTERVAL ();
Once the interval paritioning is turned off for the table, the existing already created partitions will function further as range partitions.

You can investigate the table design and try incrementing the INTERVAL value to a higher value if the application logic permits.

