#################################################################################################################################################################

The size of the database is the space the files physically consume on disk. You can find this with: 

select sum(bytes)/1024/1024 size_in_mb from dba_data_files;


But not all this space is necessarily allocated. There could be sections of these files that are not used. 

You can find the total space that is used with: 

select sum(bytes)/1024/1024 "MB", sum(bytes)/1024/1024/1024 "GB", sum(bytes)/1024/1024/1024/1024 "TB" from dba_segments;


You can break this down by user by running: 

select owner, sum(bytes)/1024/1024 Size_MB from dba_segments
group  by owner;


#################################################################################################################################################################
//to ensure no diskgroups  are to cross 85% used. Crossing this boundary will raise traps for second and third line.
select NAME as DISK_GROUP, TOTAL_MB,FREE_MB,COLD_USED_MB,free_mb/total_mb*100 as percentage_free from v$asm_diskgroup;



// to check that the total storage will not exceed the MAX_PDB_STORAGE.
Please also run for databases in PDBs
select PROPERTY_VALUE/1024/1024/1024 as PDB_SIZE_IN_GB FROM database_properties WHERE property_name = 'MAX_PDB_STORAGE';


