//  to display the contents of the optimizer environment used to build the execution plan of a SQL cursor
v$sql_optimizer_env


// Query to check which optimizer values are set for particular SQL_ID 
select ADDRESS,HASH_VALUE,SQL_ID,NAME,ISDEFAULT,VALUE from  v$sql_optimizer_env where sql_id='&sql_id';


// If SQL is not picking SPB, following can be the reasons:
•renaming indexes (or any object used in the SPM plan)
•changing the index type (from b-tree to local for example) but not reversing the index
•changing the leading columns of the index
•using a different value of NLS_SORT (compared to the one used during SPM plan capture)
•using a different value of NLS_LANG (compared to the one used during SPM plan capture)
•And others situations I am not aware of

 

It might seem strange that changing an index name will affect the reproducibility of the SPM plan because when 
reproducing the SPM plan Oracle uses the index columns name and not the index name itself. However, the index name is used 
by the algorithm generating the phv2. Therefore, if you change the index name than you will break the phv2 value and you will 
then not be able to use the SPM plan even thought the CBO plan might be the same

URL: https://community.oracle.com/thread/3695707?start=0&tstart=0


