################################################################################################################################################################
OGG-00519 and ORA-02149 goldengate error solution

Replicat fails with OGG-00519 ORA-02149 for drop interval partition with system generated name. (Doc ID 1954807.1) 

Applies to: 
 Oracle GoldenGate - Version 11.2.1.0.3 to 12.1.3.0.0 [Release 11.2 to 12.1]
Information in this document applies to any platform.

Symptoms

Replicat fails with OGG-00519 ORA-02149 for drop interval partition with system generated name.


alter table <schema>.<table> drop partition <partition name> UPDATE GLOBAL INDEXES ;


2014-12-09 10:07:27 ERROR OGG-00519 Fatal error executing DDL replication: error [Error code [2149], ORA-02149: Specified partition 
does not exist SQL alter table <schema>.<table> drop partition <partition name> UPDATE GLOBAL INDEXES /* GOLDENGATE_DDL_REPLICATION */], 
no error handler present.

 

Changes

 

Cause

 

DDL replication is unaffected by interval partitioning, because the DDL is implicit 
As this is system generated name, there is no way replicat can convert this to target.

This seems to be a limitation and we logged a documentation bug for this issue.

BUG 20182766 - Replicat errors while dropping system generated interval partition name fixed in 12.1.2.1.0

 

Solution

 

Drop the partition by partition range rather than partition name.

Example statement to drop the partition on source.

SQL> alter table <table> drop partition for (20); 
SQL> ALTER TABLE <table> DROP PARTITION FOR (TO_DATE('3-3-2004','DD-MM-YYYY'));


OR add below parameter in replicat and start the replicat 

DDLERROR 2149 ignore 

#############################################################################################################################################################
LOCAL index on primary key 

https://asktom.oracle.com/pls/apex/f?p=100:11:::::P11_QUESTION_ID:18741227819236#:~:text=the%20primary%20key%20index%20can,the%20primary%20key%20is%20ACCT_ID.


You Asked
Tom,

I have a table accounts which has 80 million records (OLTP system). I would like to partition the table by acct_by_date column.


I will be going with range partition and global indexes. My concern is regd the primary key acct_id. The index that will be created for primary key will be local or global and what should i opt for?

Thanks
Rahul.

and Tom said...
the primary key index can ONLY be local IF and ONLY IF, the primary key is in fact the (or part of the) partition key.

Hence, in this case, you are partitioning by ACCT_BY_DATE, but the primary key is ACCT_ID. The index used for the enforcement of that primary key *must* be a global index - there is no chance of using a LOCAL.

The reason -- if you think about the local index -- the value ACCT_ID = 55 could be in ANY PARTITION. Hence, any insert would have to check ALL local partitions for 55 and lock each index partition to ensure no one even tries to insert ACCT_ID=55 right now. It would be a huge scalability inhibitor as only one person at a time could insert/update or delete the primary key.

In a globally partitioned index, the value 55 can be in exactly ONE partition. We just treat that partition like we would any normal index and can achieve multiple insert/update/delete operations at the same time.


#############################################################################################################################################################


